-# pstack
+# pstack for Pi (pi-pstack)
 
-i'm [poteto](https://x.com/poteto). i'm not a president or ceo, but i've worked with millions of lines of code at Meta, Netflix, and Cursor. i'm also on the react core team where i help build and maintain react compiler.
+pi-pstack is pstack 0.15.36 for Pi 1.5.0: rigorous agent workflows (`/poteto-mode` and its playbooks), the principle skills, and the review, research, and verification skills, plus the full soft-dependency team-kit set (`deslop`, `control-cli`, `control-ui`, CI/PR helpers, thermo-nuclear review, and the rest) vendored in so you do not install a separate team-kit package. A small extension provides the host features the skills call by name: `/name` skill commands, `/mode`, `/plan`, the `Task` subagent tool with local worktree workers, forks, and `agent_status`, `TodoWrite`, `AskQuestion`, `GenerateImage`, `SwitchMode`, the model rule, `/loop`, subscriptions and webhook routines, and `visualize`.
 
-there's a growing sense that ai writes too much slop code. i agree. i don't want to ship like a team of twenty slop artists. throughput without quality is not a goal i aspire to. if you want to go fast, go deep first. 
+## Get set up
 
-**pstack is my answer.** these are the same skills i use every day to ship high quality code at Cursor. this turns cursor into a real engineering team. the goal is not to maximize loc, in fact it's the opposite. pstack helps you write less, but higher quality code.
+1. Clone the repository and install the package from the checkout. Pi loads a local package in place, so keep the checkout where it is.
 
-**pstack gives you fearless parallelism.** when you can go deep on one agent and trust it to write good, verifiable code, you can truly parallelize with confidence. start multiple agents up with `poteto-mode` and trust that they'll apply rigorous engineering principles to their work.
+   ```sh
+   git clone https://github.com/just-joshn/pi-customizations.git
+   cd pi-customizations
+   bun install
+   pi install ./extensions/pi-pstack
+   ```
 
-**cursor gives you the best of all worlds.** every frontier model has its strengths and weaknesses. use any model with pstack. in fact, many of my skills use multi-model workflows to take advantage of each model's unique strengths.
+   Pi does not install dependencies for a local package, so `bun install` (or `npm install` in `extensions/pi-pstack`) fetches `@anthropic-ai/sandbox-runtime` for the read-only shell (readonly subagents and `/plan`) and `yaml` for plan metadata. If sandbox-runtime is missing, a read-only shell command fails instead of running unsandboxed. On Linux the sandbox also needs `bubblewrap`, `socat`, and `ripgrep` (`sudo apt install bubblewrap socat ripgrep`), and Ubuntu 24.04 and later need the user-namespace change under Limits. macOS uses the built-in `sandbox-exec`. pi-pstack needs Node 22.19 or newer. To try it for one session without installing, run `pi -e ./extensions/pi-pstack`. To install the published package instead, run `pi install npm:@justjoshn/pi-pstack`.
+   Installing loads pi-pstack's tools and system-prompt sections at once, but no skill runs until you invoke it. 61 workflow skills stay out of the model's automatic skill list. Setup, create-skill, and the team-kit shipping and review skills (`deslop`, `control-cli`, `control-ui`, CI and PR helpers, and the rest of that set) can load from your words alone.
+2. Run `/setup-pstack`. It lists the models the `Task` tool can use, asks for a reasoning budget, maps a model to each role, and writes the model rule. A new rule applies from the next request.
+3. Start real work with `/poteto-mode`, a goal, and a check that can pass or fail.
 
-fork it. improve it. make it yours. PRs are welcome! 
+The guide in [`docs/guide/`](docs/guide/01-setup.md) walks through setup, `/poteto-mode`, understanding code, building, verifying, overnight runs, and making pstack yours.
 
-## install
+Remove the package with `pi remove ./extensions/pi-pstack`. Its state lives in `~/.pi/agent/pstack/`, or `$PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR/pstack/` when that variable is set (written `<pstack dir>` below): the model rule, routine credential files under `routines/`, and one folder per workspace under `workspaces/` with subagent transcripts, agent stores, projects, artifacts, and isolated-worker worktrees. Delete that directory to remove the state too.
 
-```bash
-/add-plugin pstack
-```
-
-in claude code:
-
-```bash
-/plugin marketplace add cursor/plugins
-/plugin install pstack@cursor-plugins
-```
-
-claude code leaves auto-update off for third-party marketplaces, so pstack stays on the version you installed. to turn it on, run `/plugin`, open the **marketplaces** tab, select `cursor-plugins`, and choose **enable auto-update**. to update once by hand, run `claude plugin update pstack@cursor-plugins`.
-
-claude code prefixes plugin commands and agents, so `/poteto-mode` is `/pstack:poteto-mode`. the [claude code adapter](./docs/harness/claude-code.md) maps every other cursor name. in claude code, `/pstack:setup-pstack` writes your model choices to `~/.claude/rules/pstack-models.md`, and a step that needs a cursor-only feature, such as bugbot or automations, is skipped with a one-line note.
-
-claude code installs a generated copy of pstack, including this readme and the guide, from [`harness/pstack/`](https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/main/harness/pstack) at the repository root. after you change pstack, run `node scripts/generate-pstack-harness-trees.mjs` from the repository root and commit the result. ci fails while the generated copy is out of date.
