-when invoked it:
+| `/poteto-mode <task>` | The skill attaches to that one message. Every skill Pi loaded works this way: pstack's (`/how`, `/why`, `/arena`, `/interrogate`, `/principle-prove-it-works`, `/deslop`) and your own, such as a `/<handle>-mode` skill from `/automate-me`. `/skill:<name>` works too. |
+| `/mode poteto-mode [task]` | Keeps the skill in context on every turn until you leave the mode: the skill rides every request in its own system-prompt section, which Pi re-sends only when it changes. The first request after entry says the user entered the mode, later requests carry a reminder that names the skill file, and the request after `/mode off` or a switch to another mode says the user left it. The footer shows a badge with the skill's `icon` and `color` metadata, such as a yellow `♛ mode: poteto-mode` (other skills get `⚡`). `/mode off` exits and `/mode` reports. Any loaded skill can back a mode. |
+| `/plan [task]` | Plan mode: the agent researches with read-only tools (`read`, `grep`, `find`, `ls`, the sandboxed `bash_readonly`, `AskQuestion`, `TodoWrite`, `agent_status`, `Task`, whose launches run readonly: a fresh one runs here with no worktree, and a resume or fork stays where its agent ran, and `stop_agent`), asks its clarifying questions early, and presents the plan with `CreatePlan` (`plan`, `name`, `overview`, `todos`, `is_project`, `phases`). `CreatePlan` writes the plan file under `<pstack dir>/plans/` (`<name>-<8 hex>.plan.md`: the chat id in a comment, the todos as front matter, then the plan), one file per plan name in a chat. Todo dependencies and project phases stay in the file. Building imports the file's unfinished root todos into the session, including edits made during review. Worktree builders import their own lists before the first model request and retain the associated plan on resume. Later writable `TodoWrite` status updates synchronize those root todos without rewriting other metadata or the plan body. A readonly resume keeps its todo changes inside the session. `edit` and `write` work on markdown files only, so the agent refines the plan in its file. After a run that saved a plan, an interactive session asks `Yes, build locally`, `Yes, build in cloud` (in a git repository: a background isolated worker builds it on its own branch), or `No, propose changes`. `/plan build` builds the saved plan too, and `/plan off` restores your tools. A bare `/plan` enters plan mode. The footer shows `plan`. |
+| `/loop 30m <prompt>` | Runs the prompt now, then again every 30 minutes from each fire, so a long run does not push the next tick out. A tick that comes due while a run is still going fires once when that run settles; several missed ticks still fire once. The interval can come first or last, written as `30m`, `10 minutes`, or `every 10 minutes`. Without an interval the agent picks each wake with the `Loop` tool, and the loop ends when it picks none and no background subagent is still running. Different prompts create independent loops. The same prompt replaces its schedule. `/loop` lists them. `/loop stop` ends the only loop, or `/loop stop <id>` selects one when several are running. Escape during a run ends the session's loops. `Loop` results include the loop ID in text and details; pass `loop_id` to wake, stop, or replace one of several loops. The prompt can be a skill command, such as `/loop 1h /poteto-mode run the audit tick`. When a playbook says to arm `/loop`, the agent starts it with the `Loop` tool. Intervals go up to 24 days. |
+| `/routines` | Lists webhook routines with their URL, credential file, and status. |
+| `/setup-pstack` | Configures the per-role models. |
+| `/poteto-help <question>` | Answers a question about pstack and hands you a prompt to send. |
 
