-help:              /poteto-help which skill should i use to review this branch?
+| `Task` (`description`, `prompt`, `subagent_type`, `model`, `run_in_background`, `resume`, `fork`, `fork_turns`, `readonly`, `file_attachments`, `environment`, `cloud_base_branch`) | Starts a separate `pi --mode rpc` process per subagent, so this Pi process can steer and abort it. Foreground calls return the response and an agent ID. Background calls return at once, and a `<system_notification>` with a `<task>` block arrives after your turn ends, titled by the first line of the prompt (80 characters at most) and followed by a request to do any follow-up work; the notification card shows the call's `description`. Print and JSON runs wait for pending notifications before exiting, unless aborted. Several calls in one message run in parallel. A subagent's own subagents cannot spawn more, and every subagent is told to spawn none unless asked. A run lock per agent ID keeps out a second writer, even from another Pi process. A token-directory lease serializes stale-lock recovery while the PID file identifies the transcript writer. Children inherit the parent's project-trust decision. Foreground usage counts toward the session totals. The Task card leads with the call's `description`, then the agent type and the model that ran, and while a foreground child works it shows its last few tool steps and latest line instead of a blank wait. |
+| `resume` | Sends a follow-up to an idle agent with its context, model, type, and directory. The root chat can resume or fork a descendant that a worktree worker started inside that worktree, and the descendant still runs where it first ran. On an agent running in this Pi process, it delivers the prompt as a steer and returns at once. A readonly or plan-mode call cannot steer a writable agent. An agent running in another Pi process is refused. This is how a playbook "messages" a worker. Pi's own `/resume` command reopens a chat session instead. |
+| `fork` | Starts a new agent whose transcript begins as a copy of an idle agent's transcript, in that agent's directory (its worktree for an isolated worker), so it sees the built checkout and uncommitted changes. Type, model, and readonly default from the source; readonly can be added, never lifted. `fork_turns: N` (only with `fork`) copies just the first N user turns. `fork` excludes `resume`, `environment`, and `cloud_base_branch`. Pi's own `/fork` command branches the chat session instead. |
+| `stop_agent` | `agent_id`. Stops a running subagent. One this Pi process owns gets an RPC abort, then a kill after a grace period. One another of your Pi processes owns gets a signal to the live pid in its run lock. An idle or finished agent is left alone, and the result says so. |
+| `agent_status` | Reads a subagent's state (`running` while its run lock names a live pid, `idle`, or `missing`), type, model, directory, branch, times, and last reply, without waking it. It also shows whether the agent is readonly, the agent it was forked from, its pid while running, and its transcript. Without `agent_id` it lists the workspace's 20 newest agents. In the root chat that includes every descendant, isolated workers too. |
+| Subagent types | The built-in `generalPurpose` (default) and `explore` (a file-search specialist the caller tells how thorough to be, with `grep`, `find`, and `ls` on and no `Task`), then `poteto-agent` (a background delegate that reads poteto-mode first), `ci-watcher` (a background PR check watcher), `comment-sicko`, and `thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review`, the last four read from `agents/*.md`. Unknown types fail with the list. |
+| Models | A Pi model spec such as `claude-opus-5-5:xhigh` or `xai/grok-4.7:high`, model parameters such as `claude-opus-5-5[effort=high]` or `grok-4.7[fast=true]` (picks a listed `-fast` id of that model), `fast` for the parent model's listed `-fast` variant when it has one, else the parent model, or `inherit`, `inherit-parent`, `auto`, or nothing for the parent model. A model Pi does not list, or a thinking level the model does not support, fails with the allowed list. A bare model id that several providers offer resolves to the parent chat's provider when it offers that id, and otherwise fails with the choices. Older rules that wrote `claude-opus-5-5-xhigh` still resolve. The system prompt's `pstack_subagents` section lists the types, the parent model, and the models with their levels. |
+| Isolated workers (`environment: "cloud"`) | The subagent runs in a new git worktree at `<pstack dir>/workspaces/<workspace folder>/worktrees/<agent id>` on branch `pstack/<agent id>`, from `cloud_base_branch` (`origin/<branch>` when that remote-tracking ref exists, else a local branch or other ref) or your HEAD commit, so uncommitted changes do not reach it. The result and the card name the branch to merge, and `resume` and `fork` run in the same worktree. It fails clearly outside a git repository, in a repository with no commits, or when the base does not resolve. The worker shares the root chat's workspace state, so it sees the same projects, stores, and subagent transcripts. It also shares this machine's ports, processes, writable caches, simulators, and credentials. Assign separate ports, output directories, and writable caches to concurrent runtime lanes. `swarm` and `orchestrate` spawn worktree workers by default. |
+| `readonly: true` | The child gets `read`, `grep`, `find`, `ls`, `TodoWrite`, and a read-only `bash`, and no MCP. That shell runs each command in an OS sandbox ([`@anthropic-ai/sandbox-runtime`](https://github.com/anthropics/sandbox-runtime): `sandbox-exec` on macOS, bubblewrap on Linux) that blocks every file write and all network access, so `git log` works and `touch` or `curl` fail. Where no sandbox can start, the command fails rather than running unsandboxed. Readonly is not credential isolation. The child still inherits environment variables and can read credential files through its read tools. |
+| `TodoWrite` | `todos` with `id`, `content`, `status`, and `merge`. Its description says when to keep a list, the four states, and one task in progress at a time. The list follows the session branch, shows above the editor, and rides every request's system prompt. Plan-imported lists persist as branch entries. Their dependencies survive status-only updates, and matching plan-file statuses are synchronized. |
+| `AskQuestion` | `title`, and per question `id`, `prompt`, `options` (at least two, each `id`, `label`, optional `description`), and `allow_multiple`, through Pi's select dialog. It is for decisions only the user can make, and a recommended option goes first with `(Recommended)`. Every question ends with `Other: (type to answer)`; a typed answer comes back as `other` with its text. In print and JSON runs, including subagents, each question comes back skipped. |
+| Always-applied rules | The two team-kit rules are in `pstack_host` as their shipped text on every request, alongside the same rules as hidden skills. |
+| The model rule | `<pstack dir>/pstack-models.md`, plain Markdown with one line per role, added to every request's system prompt. A `pstack-models.mdc` from an earlier version is moved there once, without its frontmatter, and kept as `pstack-models.mdc.migrated`. |
+| Subscriptions | `subscribe_github_pr` (`scope` `pr` with `prUrl`, `repo` with `repo`, or `author` with `author` and `repo`) polls with `gh` every 60 seconds and wakes the chat when a PR's state, head, mergeability, review decision, reviews, or comments change, or when a check concludes or its conclusion changes; the first poll is a baseline. `subscribe_timer` takes a `name`, a `prompt`, and a 5-field `cron` or `delaySeconds`, either with `once`; `sessionStrategy` `wake_self` queues a wake for this owning session. With `new_session`, a live owner starts a background Task and receives its notification. An offline owner permits a detached run with a recorded outcome. Both reuse the model, thinking level, and project-trust decision the timer was created with. Re-subscribing an existing timer name returns `created: false`. `list_subscriptions` and `unsubscribe` manage them. The authoritative registry is `<pstack dir>/durable/<workspace>/subscriptions.json`. Any session in that workspace can list or cancel subscriptions. Session history does not recreate cancelled or completed subscriptions. Owner-directed events wait for the creating session rather than waking another workspace session. A detached run records its own final message, shown by `list_subscriptions`. A timer does not expire unless `expiresInHours` says so (at most 30 days); `0` means no expiry. A GitHub watch keeps a 7-day default because it is this port's own surface. |
+| Webhook routines | `subscribe_webhook` (`name`, `prompt`, optional `sessionStrategy`) serves `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/routines/<slug>` (port `PSTACK_ROUTINES_PORT`, else the port this workspace last bound, else 47913, where `0` picks a free port) and keeps a random sender key in `<pstack dir>/routines/<slug>/credentials.json` (mode 0600). The result names the URL and the file, never the key. A JSON POST with `X-Automation-Key` or `Authorization: Bearer` returns 200 after durable acceptance, and 503 with `Retry-After` while that routine already holds 20 undelivered events. With the default `wake_self`, the owner receives a `[routine]` turn holding the prompt and a `<webhook_event>` block (`headers`, `body_digest`, `body`, `timestamp_ms`). With `new_session`, each accepted POST starts a fresh detached run, the way a hosted automation runs with no chat open, and records its outcome in `list_subscriptions`. HTTP 200 does not mean the run finished. Each authenticated POST is a separate event, including identical bodies. Storage failure returns 503 rather than acknowledging a lost event, and a full delivery queue refuses the new event instead of dropping an earlier accepted one. A wrong key gets 401, an unknown routine 404, a method other than POST 405, a body over 64 KiB 413, and a body that is not a JSON object 400. A routine does not expire unless `expiresInHours` says so. `make-bot-ui` builds a page on top of this. |
+| Durable supervisor | Creating a subscription auto-starts `pstack-routines-supervisor --workspace <cwd>` when it is not already running. Creation waits for that subscription to arm, and webhook creation returns the bound listener's URL. The supervisor reads the durable registry, arms timers and GitHub polls, and owns the routines port. An online owner receives native Pi continuation messages. Owner-directed events accepted while the owner is offline wait for that same session to resume. A timer or webhook routine explicitly configured with `sessionStrategy: "new_session"` starts detached work while its owner is offline. You can still start the bin yourself. For reboot survival, run `packaging/install-supervisor-unit.sh --workspace <cwd>` (idempotent; installs the launchd or systemd unit from the examples under `packaging/`). Print and JSON runs still cannot *create* subscriptions. |
+| Stores and projects | The system prompt names "the current agent's store" (per session), "the root agent's store" (the main chat's, named to every subagent however deep), and the workspace's `projects/` folder, where a program's Project is `<slug>/notes.md` (its tracker) and `<slug>/store/`. |
+| `visualize` | `title`, `kind` (`bar` or `line`), `labels`, `series` (`name`, `values`), and `unit` render a Unicode chart in the result and write a standalone SVG to the session's artifacts folder. |
+| Transcripts | The system prompt names this workspace's Pi session directory, the current session file, and this workspace's subagent transcripts. |
+| `GenerateImage` | Generates an image (`description` of at least six words, `filename`, `reference_image_paths`, `aspect_ratio`) on Pi's image models through `ctx.modelRegistry.generateImages()`. It is offered when an image model is available (for example an OpenRouter image model), not to readonly subagents or in plan mode, and saves each image to the session's artifacts folder. Without an image model, the host section tells skills to draw a Mermaid diagram instead. |
+| `SwitchMode` | `target_mode_id` (`plan` or `agent`) and `explanation`. The agent proposes entering or leaving plan mode, and the switch happens only after you confirm it. Offered in interactive and RPC sessions. |
+
+Subagent transcripts are Pi sessions in `<pstack dir>/workspaces/<workspace folder>/subagents/`, with the agent ID in each file name, next to a small record that `resume`, `fork`, and `agent_status` read. Records belong to the main chat's shared workspace state, so the root chat can act on a descendant that ran in a worktree. Children inherit your installed extensions and MCP servers, and local ones run in your working directory. Give each local subagent that writes its own git worktree, or launch it with `environment: "cloud"`, because a child process does not isolate the filesystem. A child exits on its own if its parent Pi process dies.
+
+## Limits
+
+- Worktree workers run on this machine, not remote VMs. They start from committed code only and share your installed extensions, MCP servers, and network, except that a readonly worker gets no MCP servers and no network. A worktree is not a credential boundary. Concurrent runtime lanes need separate ports, output directories, and writable caches. pi-pstack never pushes their branches, though a worker can push its own. Worktrees and branches stay until you or the worktree-cleanup playbook remove them.
+- The supervisor can accept events after chat exit, but owner-directed events wait until the owning session resumes. Explicit `new_session` timers and webhook routines can spend tokens with no human watching. Detached runs do not supply the live owner's Task notification flow. For reboot survival, run `packaging/install-supervisor-unit.sh --workspace <cwd>`. If the supervisor cannot start, an explicitly reported in-process fallback stops when that Pi process exits. A GitHub subscription restarted after downtime takes a new baseline, so changes made while nothing was polling do not wake it. Each workspace binds its own routines port. With no explicit port, a busy default falls back to an ephemeral port that the workspace then reuses and reports, so two workspaces serve routines at once. An explicit `PSTACK_ROUTINES_PORT` is a configuration: if it is taken, the routine reports that error instead of moving. The listener binds `127.0.0.1`, so a page served to other machines forwards through a local server, as `make-bot-ui` describes.
+- Event delivery is at-least-once. A process crash after Pi queues a message but before its durable acknowledgement can repeat a wake or a fresh run. Automation prompts must make external actions idempotent. Up to 20 undelivered events per subscription stay queued until delivery or cancellation, and a new event beyond that is refused rather than replacing an older accepted one. Expiry stops new occurrences rather than discarding accepted work. Failed detached executions retain their event for retry and expose the error in `list_subscriptions`. Cancelling a subscription drops pending undelivered events, but cannot undo work already handed to Pi.
+- The Benny automation pack under `automations/benny/` runs on pi-pstack subscriptions. Keep its owning Pi session running for immediate handling, or resume that session to handle parked events. It needs a Slack MCP server for its Slack steps.
+- `/plan` uses `bash_readonly` for its shell rather than replacing `bash`, so your shell settings stay as they are outside plan mode.
+- Background subagent token usage is recorded in the child's transcript and in the `usage` field of the notification's details, but does not enter the parent session's totals: Pi 1.5.0 has no public API for adding a background completion's usage to the parent totals. Native tool-result usage covers synchronous nested work, not later notifications.
+- `/reload`, `/new`, and Pi's `/resume` and `/fork` commands stop running background subagents, with a warning that names how many. Continue one later with `Task` `resume` and its agent ID.
+- A print or JSON run waits for its background subagents before it exits, with no timeout of its own, unless it is aborted.
+- Subagents start the same Pi script that runs the parent (`process.argv[1]`), or `pi` from `PATH`. Embedding the package through the SDK in another host is not supported.
+- Depth limits apply to `Task`. A writable subagent can still start `pi` through `bash`, and that process is outside the depth limit and parent watchdog.
+- A child loads your installed extensions plus this package. If you run one checkout with `-e` while a different checkout of pi-pstack is installed, the child loads both and fails on duplicate tools; use one copy, or a separate `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR`, as `live:e2e` does.
+- Subagents run headless in RPC mode, so an extension that asks for permission through a dialog cannot ask there.
+- The read-only shell sandboxes shell subprocesses only. Earlier macOS and Ubuntu CI runs do not verify the stricter Unix-socket policy. On Linux it needs `bubblewrap`, `socat`, and `ripgrep`. Ubuntu 24.04 and later restrict unprivileged user namespaces: either add an AppArmor profile that grants `userns` to `bwrap`, or run `sudo sysctl -w kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0`, which lifts the restriction for every program on the machine. Unix sockets stay blocked because a host socket service can mutate files or access the network on a command's behalf. Linux also needs sandbox-runtime's bundled `apply-seccomp` helper and permission to create its nested user, PID, and mount namespaces. A missing helper or a namespace refusal blocks the shell command. The shell never falls back to permitting host sockets. Do not treat a readonly child as a credential boundary. Environment variables and readable credential files are still available to its read tools.
+- Whether a model follows a playbook is the model's call.
+- Workspace folders follow Pi's session-folder naming, so two paths that differ only by `/` versus `-` share a folder. Resume and fork refuse an agent whose record names the other directory, except that a record created inside this workspace's own worktrees belongs to this workspace. Subscription reads and mutations also reject a registry owned by the other workspace.
+- The run lock that keeps one writer per agent ID names the process writing the transcript. A lock file without a readable pid counts as held; the error names the file to delete. `agent_status` shows such an agent as `idle`. A token-directory lease beside the PID file serializes stale-lock recovery. A dead process or an unreaped Linux zombie does not count as a live writer. Older installed versions without the lease do not participate in this protection.
+- `skills/poteto-mode/scripts/watch-pr` needs Bun and installs its one dependency on first run. `check-plan.mjs` needs only Node. `worktree-audit.sh` needs `rg` and `jq`, and works with BSD (macOS) or GNU `stat` and `date`.
+- `statechart` runs `skills/statechart/scripts/statechart` on Node 22.18 to 22.x, Node 23.6 or later, Bun 1.1.5 or later, or Deno 2.0 or later. The first run installs TLC and Java through [mise](https://mise.jdx.dev/) and needs the network. Without a SPEC it prints `Give one SPEC.tla.` and its usage, then exits 2.
+
+## Maintain it
+
+The skills, agents, guide, and automation pack are this package's own files; edit them in place. They track pstack 0.15.36 in behavior. [`docs/parity-status.md`](docs/parity-status.md) records what was reconstructed, what each host translation uses, and which differences remain host-bound. The record of the move to Pi-native text and the host features added for it is at the repository root, in `parity/pi-pstack-0.15.36/` (design and decisions); earlier records are in the root's `parity/pi-pstack-*`. Four mechanisms Pi 1.5.0 does not export keep a hand-written implementation here: [`docs/native-gaps.md`](docs/native-gaps.md) names each one, the evidence, and the public API that would replace it.
+
+```sh
+bun run typecheck
+bun run test
+bun run test:statechart                               # full compiler suite; set TLA2TOOLS and JAVA for an existing verified toolchain
+bun run live:e2e -- --model deepseek/deepseek-flash      # real-model scenarios; needs credentials and gives the model under test your real auth.json (see the script header)
 ```
 
-</details>
